+\subsection{Maxwell--SST Dynamical Field Closure: Transverse-Mode, Displacement-Current and Gravitational Energy-Deficit Gates}
+\label{sec:rt_maxwell_dynamical_field_closure}
+
+\subsubsection{Status, source discipline, and purpose}
+
+\textbf{[ORTHODOX HISTORICAL INPUT / SST RESEARCH-TRACK TEST].}
+Maxwell's 1865 field programme separates conduction from changing electric
+displacement, treats field energy as residing in the surrounding field, derives
+transverse propagating electromagnetic disturbances from the closed field
+system, and explicitly asks whether gravitation can be represented by a medium
+whose energy is reduced in the presence of a resultant gravitational field
+\cite{Maxwell1865DynamicalField}.  The present section adopts none of Maxwell's
+literal nineteenth-century ether microphysics.  It uses only these structural
+questions as negative tests on SST closures.
+
+The three primary questions are:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item does a transverse radiative branch emerge from the declared
+    unconstrained SST dynamics without applying a solenoidal projector to the
+    data being tested;
+    \item can the coarse-grained polarization and its current be reconstructed
+    from an independently resolved SST internal coordinate while satisfying
+    charge continuity on held-out data;
+    \item does one and the same positive SST Hamiltonian generate an attractive
+    interaction through a negative \emph{interaction-energy difference}, with
+    the force equal to the derivative of that energy and also to an independent
+    stress/momentum-flux reconstruction?
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\textbf{[NON-IDENTIFICATION GUARD].}
+The equal dimensions
+$[\delta p_{\rm swirl}]=[\delta\rhoE]=\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}$ do not imply
+$\delta p_{\rm swirl}=\delta\rhoE$.  Likewise, the Maxwell displacement-current
+analogy does not identify a particular Kelvin, twist, writhe, core, or link mode
+with electric polarization before an independent coupling calculation has done
+so.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate DFC--T: no-projector transverse-mode theorem}
+\label{subsec:rt_dfc_transverse_gate}
+
+\paragraph{Reference decomposition.}
+Before imposing Eq.~\eqref{eq:div_free_Aeff}, write the effective displacement
+as a Helmholtz decomposition
+\begin{align}
+    \mathbf A_{\rm eff}
+    =
+    \mathbf A_T+\nabla\chi,
+    \qquad
+    \nabla\cdot\mathbf A_T=0.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_helmholtz_A}
+\end{align}
+For the existing quadratic bridge action, now interpreted temporarily on the
+unprojected field,
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal L_{\rm rad}^{\rm unproj}
+    =
+    \frac{\rhoEff}{2}|\partial_t\mathbf A_{\rm eff}|^2
+    -
+    \frac{K_\gamma}{2}|\nabla\times\mathbf A_{\rm eff}|^2,
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_unprojected_lagrangian}
+\end{align}
+the Euler--Lagrange equation is
+\begin{align}
+    \rhoEff\,\partial_t^2\mathbf A_{\rm eff}
+    +
+    K_\gamma\,\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf A_{\rm eff})
+    =0.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_unprojected_eom}
+\end{align}
+The transverse and longitudinal parts therefore obey
+\begin{align}
+    \rhoEff\,\partial_t^2\mathbf A_T
+    -K_\gamma\nabla^2\mathbf A_T
+    &=0,
+    \\
+    \rhoEff\,\nabla\partial_t^2\chi
+    &=0.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_TL_reference_equations}
+\end{align}
+Thus the quadratic bridge itself has a finite-frequency transverse wave branch
+and only a nonpropagating longitudinal zero branch.  This calculation is a
+\textbf{reference theorem of the declared bridge action}, not a microscopic SST
+derivation.  Maxwell's own 1865 discussion similarly establishes transverse
+propagation within his field equations while explicitly noting that extra
+unobserved microscopic inertia could, in principle, alter the status of normal
+vibrations \cite{Maxwell1865DynamicalField}.
+
+\paragraph{Microscopic theorem target.}
+For every candidate microdynamical realization, compute normal modes or a
+space--time spectrum \emph{before} any transverse projection.  For a mode with
+wave vector $\mathbf k$ and complex amplitude $\widetilde{\mathbf A}$ define
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal R_L
+    :=
+    \frac{|\widehat{\mathbf k}\cdot\widetilde{\mathbf A}|^2}
+    {|\widetilde{\mathbf A}|^2},
+    \qquad
+    \widehat{\mathbf k}:=\frac{\mathbf k}{|\mathbf k|}.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_longitudinal_fraction}
+\end{align}
+A propagating transverse branch is fit without fixing the speed target,
+\begin{align}
+    \omega_T^2
+    =
+    a_T k^2+b_T.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_blind_transverse_dispersion}
+\end{align}
+Define the dimensionless infrared gap statistic
+\begin{align}
+    g_T
+    :=
+    \frac{|b_T|}
+    {a_T k_{\rm med}^2}.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_transverse_gap_statistic}
+\end{align}
+The primary gate is not $\mathcal R_L=0$ for every zero-frequency coordinate;
+it is the absence of a \emph{finite-frequency longitudinal radiative branch}
+within the declared operational band.
+
+\paragraph{Preregistered DFC--T thresholds.}
+A strict campaign is valid only when its metadata declare
+\begin{align}
+    \texttt{projector\_applied=false},\qquad
+    \texttt{gauge\_reduced\_input=false},\qquad
+    \texttt{divergence\_constraint\_enforced=false}.
+\end{align}
+For modes above five percent of the maximum resolved frequency, require
+\begin{align}
+    \mathcal R_{L,\rm rad}^{\rm power}
+    &\le 10^{-3},
+    \\
+    R_T^2
+    &\ge0.995,
+    \\
+    g_T
+    &\le0.02,
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_T_thresholds}
+\end{align}
+and reject the declared radiation closure if a longitudinally dominated mode
+$\mathcal R_L\ge0.8$ propagates with phase speed at least $0.2$ times the fitted
+transverse speed over the same infrared window.  These numerical thresholds are
+analysis tolerances, not fitted physical constants.
+
+\paragraph{Energy partition cross-check.}
+The Hamiltonian density of the existing quadratic radiation bridge is
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \mathcal H_{\rm rad}
+    =
+    \frac{\rhoEff}{2}|\partial_t\mathbf A_{\rm eff}|^2
+    +
+    \frac{K_\gamma}{2}|\nabla\times\mathbf A_{\rm eff}|^2
+    \ge0
+    }
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_radiation_hamiltonian}
+\end{align}
+for $\rhoEff>0$ and $K_\gamma>0$.  A monochromatic transverse solution of
+Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_dfc_TL_reference_equations} with
+$\omega^2=(K_\gamma/\rhoEff)k^2$ satisfies the cycle-average relation
+\begin{align}
+    \left\langle
+    \frac{\rhoEff}{2}|\partial_t\mathbf A_T|^2
+    \right\rangle
+    =
+    \left\langle
+    \frac{K_\gamma}{2}|\nabla\times\mathbf A_T|^2
+    \right\rangle.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_wave_equipartition}
+\end{align}
+When both energy channels are independently available, the blind numerical
+cross-check requires the median absolute logarithmic ratio
+$|\ln(E_{\rm kin}/E_{\rm el})|\le0.05$.  Failure rejects the quadratic-wave
+closure for that dataset, but does not by itself reject all possible nonlinear
+SST radiation models.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate DFC--D: displacement-current micro-closure}
+\label{subsec:rt_dfc_displacement_gate}
+
+\paragraph{Candidate microscopic coordinate.}
+Let $\boldsymbol{\xi}_{\rm pol}$ be an independently resolved generalized
+polarization coordinate of a declared SST carrier or link subsystem.  Its
+physical identity must be chosen from the mode classification programme, not
+from electromagnetic target fitting.  The leading constitutive test is
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \mathbf P_{\rm swirl}
+    =
+    \mathsf K_P\boldsymbol{\xi}_{\rm pol}
+    +O(|\boldsymbol{\xi}_{\rm pol}|^2)
+    }
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_P_microclosure}
+\end{align}
+with a constant scalar, diagonal, or full tensor $\mathsf K_P$ selected
+\emph{before} the campaign.  For a displacement coordinate,
+$[\boldsymbol{\xi}_{\rm pol}]=\mathrm m$ and therefore
+$[\mathsf K_P]=\mathrm{C\,m^{-3}}$ when
+$[\mathbf P]=\mathrm{C\,m^{-2}}$.
+
+The associated bound charge and polarization current are
+\begin{align}
+    \rho_{\rm b}
+    &:=-\nabla\cdot\mathbf P_{\rm swirl},
+    \\
+    \mathbf J_{\rm pol}
+    &:=\partial_t\mathbf P_{\rm swirl},
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_bound_charge_current}
+\end{align}
+which imply
+\begin{align}
+    \partial_t\rho_{\rm b}
+    +\nabla\cdot\mathbf J_{\rm pol}=0.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_bound_continuity}
+\end{align}
+The identity in Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_dfc_bound_continuity} is not a useful
+falsifier if all three fields are algebraically generated from the same stored
+$\mathbf P_{\rm swirl}$.  A valid test therefore requires independent solver or
+measurement channels for $\boldsymbol{\xi}_{\rm pol}$, $\mathbf P_{\rm swirl}$,
+$\mathbf J_{\rm pol}$, and $\rho_{\rm b}$.
+
+\paragraph{Frequency-domain blind observables.}
+For a Fourier component proportional to
+$e^{i(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x-\omega t)}$, independently reconstructed channels
+must satisfy
+\begin{align}
+    \mathbf J_{\rm pol}
+    &\stackrel{?}{=}
+    -i\omega\mathbf P_{\rm swirl},
+    \\
+    \rho_{\rm b}
+    &\stackrel{?}{=}
+    -i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf P_{\rm swirl},
+    \\
+    -i\omega\rho_{\rm b}
+    +i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf J_{\rm pol}
+    &\stackrel{?}{=}0.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_frequency_current_relations}
+\end{align}
+Fit $\mathsf K_P$ on a deterministic $70\%$ training subset, freeze it, and
+score the remaining $30\%$ without refitting.  Define normalized residuals
+$\epsilon_P$, $\epsilon_J$, $\epsilon_\rho$, and $\epsilon_{\rm cont}$ using the
+root-mean-square norm divided by the corresponding observed RMS scale.
+
+\paragraph{Preregistered DFC--D thresholds.}
+A valid independent-channel campaign requires
+\begin{align}
+    \epsilon_P^{\rm holdout}&\le0.05,
+    &
+    \epsilon_J&\le0.05,
+    &
+    \epsilon_\rho&\le0.05,
+    &
+    \epsilon_{\rm cont}&\le0.05,
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_D_thresholds}
+\end{align}
+plus a coefficient-stability residual not exceeding $0.10$ when the training
+set is split into two deterministic halves.  Any electromagnetic SI
+normalization target is opened only after these residuals and the fitted
+$\mathsf K_P$ have been frozen.
+
+\subsubsection{Gate DFC--G: gravitational interaction-energy deficit}
+\label{subsec:rt_dfc_gravity_energy_gate}
+
+\paragraph{Historical trigger and SST distinction.}
+Maxwell noted that a medium account of universal attraction would require the
+field energy near gravitating matter to be smaller than the undisturbed value,
+while the absolute intrinsic energy must remain positive
+\cite{Maxwell1865DynamicalField}.  SST has an independent pressure/stress
+channel, but this historical observation does \emph{not} license the local
+identity $\delta\rhoE=\delta p_{\rm swirl}$.
+
+\paragraph{Hamiltonian definition.}
+Let $\mathcal X_d$ be a fully relaxed two-source SST state with source separation
+$d$, all numerical and boundary settings held fixed, and let
+$H_{\rm SST}[\mathcal X]$ be one declared Hamiltonian functional.  Define
+\begin{align}
+    U_{\rm int}(d)
+    :=
+    H_{\rm SST}[\mathcal X_d]
+    -
+    H_{\rm SST}[\mathcal X_\infty].
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_interaction_energy}
+\end{align}
+The radial Hamiltonian force, with increasing $d$ taken as the positive outward
+direction, is
+\begin{align}
+    F_H(d)
+    :=
+    -\frac{dU_{\rm int}}{dd}.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_hamiltonian_force}
+\end{align}
+An attractive energy-deficit branch therefore has
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    U_{\rm int}(d)<0,
+    \qquad
+    \frac{dU_{\rm int}}{dd}>0,
+    \qquad
+    F_H(d)<0.
+    }
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_gravity_sign_triplet}
+\end{align}
+These are statements about an interaction-energy \emph{difference}; they do
+not require negative absolute energy density.
+
+\paragraph{Independent force channel.}
+The same states must provide an independently reconstructed force
+$F_\sigma(d)$ from a declared surface-stress, momentum-flux, or constraint-force
+calculation that does not numerically differentiate the stored total-energy
+column.  Define
+\begin{align}
+    \epsilon_F
+    :=
+    \frac{\operatorname{RMS}[F_\sigma-F_H]}
+    {\max(\operatorname{RMS}[F_\sigma],F_{\rm floor})}.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_force_energy_residual}
+\end{align}
+A positive absolute-energy guard additionally requires the minimum sampled
+absolute energy density to satisfy
+\begin{align}
+    \rho_{E,\min}
+    \ge
+    -10^{-10}\rho_{E,\rm scale},
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_positive_absolute_energy_guard}
+\end{align}
+where the negative allowance is numerical roundoff tolerance only.
+
+\paragraph{Preregistered DFC--G thresholds.}
+For the finite-separation samples, require
+\begin{align}
+    \Pr[U_{\rm int}<0]&\ge0.90,
+    &
+    \Pr[dU_{\rm int}/dd>0]&\ge0.90,
+    \\
+    \Pr[F_\sigma<0]&\ge0.90,
+    &
+    \epsilon_F&\le0.10,
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_G_thresholds}
+\end{align}
+together with Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_dfc_positive_absolute_energy_guard}.  Failure of
+one condition rejects the declared mechanical gravity closure for that source
+family.
+
+\paragraph{Target-blind far-field exponents.}
+Before any Newtonian comparison, fit
+\begin{align}
+    |U_{\rm int}|&\propto d^{-n_U},
+    &
+    |F_\sigma|&\propto d^{-n_F}.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_blind_gravity_exponents}
+\end{align}
+The values $(n_U,n_F)$ are reported and hashed with the blind result but are not
+used in DFC--G pass/fail.  Only after the result is frozen may the orthodox
+inverse-distance/inverse-square targets $(1,2)$ be revealed as a secondary
+comparison.  This prevents the Newtonian exponents from entering the solver or
+fit-selection loop.
+
+\subsubsection{Frequency-dependent constitutive guard}
+
+Maxwell's historical programme also warns against extending a constitutive
+response measured at one time scale to arbitrarily rapid oscillations.  SST
+therefore records the generic possibility
+\begin{align}
+    K_\gamma&\to K_\gamma(\omega,\mathbf k),
+    &
+    \mathsf K_P&\to\mathsf K_P(\omega,\mathbf k),
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_dispersive_constitutive_guard}
+\end{align}
+unless scale independence is separately demonstrated.  A quasi-static coil or
+material-response coefficient must not be promoted to the R-phase/optical sector
+by extrapolation alone.
+
+\subsubsection{Blindness, split discipline, and falsification logic}
+
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item All thresholds in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:rt_dfc_T_thresholds},
+    \eqref{eq:rt_dfc_D_thresholds}, and \eqref{eq:rt_dfc_G_thresholds} are frozen
+    before opening target comparisons.
+    \item The analysis code must emit the SHA-256 hash of the preregistration
+    file and of the frozen result bundle.
+    \item Train/hold-out selection for DFC--D is deterministic from a seed stored
+    in the preregistration file; changing the seed after seeing results is a new
+    campaign.
+    \item A transverse projector, gauge-reduced eigensystem, or algebraically
+    generated ``independent'' polarization/current channel makes the relevant
+    gate \texttt{INVALID}, not \texttt{PASS}.
+    \item Per-dataset coefficient retuning after hold-out inspection is forbidden.
+    \item Passing all three primary gates validates only the tested closure.  It
+    does not derive the elementary charge, $\varepsilon_0$, $\mu_0$, $G$, or the
+    complete Standard Model from SST.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\paragraph{Legacy-reference scale check.}
+Using the explicitly legacy-reference value
+$\rhoRef=7.0\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and the calibrated
+$\vchar=1.09384563\times10^6\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, the quadratic substrate scale
+is
+\begin{align}
+    \frac12\rhoRef\vchar{}^2
+    &=4.1877439\times10^5\ \mathrm{J\,m^{-3}},
+    \\
+    \frac14\rhoRef\vchar{}^2
+    &=2.0938720\times10^5\ \mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}.
+    \label{eq:rt_dfc_legacy_energy_scales}
+\end{align}
+The second value is the cycle-averaged kinetic or elastic half for a reference
+plane wave whose velocity-like amplitude equals $\vchar$.  Because
+$\rhoEff$ remains an unfixed effective response in the present Canon, these are
+normalization checks rather than absolute predictions.
+
+\paragraph{Child-level analogy.}
+A good wave test is like removing the training wheels from a bicycle: if the
+model stays transverse only because the code secretly holds it upright, nothing
+has been learned.  The gravity test is like checking a hill with two different
+instruments: the drop in stored energy and the measured pull must describe the
+same slope.
+
